preConditionHook:
// THE MARKET TURNED WITHOUT EVER REACHING THE LEVEL. Long: the lowest low of
// the last `recentLookback` bars stayed at least `untouchedTicks` ABOVE the
// lowest low of the `levelLookback` bars ending just before that window, and
// this bar closed up -- the support below was never even tested, which Brooks
// reads as strength in the buyers. Short is the mirror: the recent highest high
// stayed that far BELOW the prior window's high and this bar closed down.
//
// THE POLARITY IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE REST OF THIS SLICE. Every other level rule
// here fires when price REACHES the level; this one fires when it demonstrably
// did NOT. It is mechanized off the SAME offset window extreme as
// TouchOfRecentExtreme so the two can be compared directly.
//
// SIDES: the merged pseudocode states the SHORT (the failure to reach resistance
// above, a sell setup). It is written into the short slot here and the long slot
// carries its mirror, per the corpus convention that longCondition is the
// bullish read.
//
// THE MARGIN IS WRITTEN AS THE PAIR 'strictly short of the level AND at least N
// ticks short of it', so untouchedTicks = 0 still means the level was genuinely
// never touched rather than touched-exactly. Default 2.
//
// The level window is offset by recentLookback + 1 bars so it cannot overlap the
// recent window that is being tested against it. The offset is an input, not a
// loop variable, and it is applied to the price series itself rather than to
// the result of Highest, which would be a function-history read. Both extremes
// mirror Extremes(): seeded at the window's first bar, scanning the rest,
// strict and toleranced (el_gt / el_lt), EL source.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry, never a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: recentLookback + levelLookback.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
levelHigh = high[(int)recentLookback + 1];
levelLow = low[(int)recentLookback + 1];
for (ii = (int)recentLookback + 2; ii <= (int)recentLookback + (int)levelLookback; ++ii) {
    if (el_gt(high[ii], levelHigh)) levelHigh = high[ii];
    if (el_lt(low[ii], levelLow)) levelLow = low[ii];
}
recentHigh = high[0];
recentLow = low[0];
for (ii = 1; ii <= (int)recentLookback - 1; ++ii) {
    if (el_gt(high[ii], recentHigh)) recentHigh = high[ii];
    if (el_lt(low[ii], recentLow)) recentLow = low[ii];
}
